<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Caucasian Imamate</h1><p class="st-meta">1828 CE – 1859 CE · capital Vedeno</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Islamic state of Imam Shamil in Dagestan and Chechnya, which fought the Russian Empire for three decades before surrendering at Gunib in 1859.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1820s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teachers of the Naqshbandi order preached holy war and strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> against both Russian rule and local customary law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi-muhammad">Ghazi Muhammad</a> was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> around 1828-1829 and led the first campaigns until he was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimry">Gimry</a> in 1832. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamzat-bek">Hamzat Bek</a> was assassinated in 1834, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Shamil</a> became imam.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Shamil</a> built a centralised theocracy across mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechnya">Chechnya</a>, dividing it into districts under naibs, levying taxes, raising a standing army and replacing customary law with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>. He survived the Russian siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-akhulgo">Akhulgo</a> in 1839 and defeated a large Russian expedition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dargo">Dargo</a> in 1845. The capital moved, settling at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedeno">Vedeno</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Exhausted by war and Russian advances through the forests, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> collapsed. In 1859 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Shamil</a> surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunib">Gunib</a> and lived in honourable exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, dying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 1871. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed the region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/caucasian-imamate.jpg" alt="Caucasian Imamate" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: Mega programmer at English Wikipedia, Takhirgeran Umar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imamat_1854.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1828 CE – 1859 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vedeno</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ghazi Muhammad, Hamzat Bek, Shamil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Avar; Chechen; Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>North Caucasian Imamate, Imamate of Dagestan and Chechnya, Imamate of Shamil</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=caucasian-imamate" aria-label="Caucasian Imamate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5793 652 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Caucasian Imamate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6113 908L6131 921M6113 908L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6131 921h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6113 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6113 908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vedeno. <a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=caucasian-imamate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

